I would like a smokeping-like program, written in go as a single executable, that does
network monitoring.  Smokeping is kind of a handful to manage because it is perl, but also
one downside is that it only does a burst of pings every minute.

I'd like something that has the following features: It can take multiple destinations, it
can ping every X seconds, maybe defaulting to 5 (but potentially even fractional seconds
like 0.1), we might want a "default timeout" after which we consider a packet lost (for
display purposes, we would still record a >timeout packet if we receive it later), it has
a web interface that can display ping latency and loss graphs, with drill-down.
That is the core set of features, additional features I'd like are the ability to specify
network interfaces on the local machine and for it to monitor interface statistics like
errors and lost packets and report them, and also to periodically send a trace route burst
of packets to record the route the packets are taking to reach the destination, and record
changes in that route.

A tricky consideration will be where to store that data.  smokeping uses RRD files as the
data store, but those can be tricky because they flush the discs for reliability and that
causes a LOT of disc contention, even on SSDs.  I probably want to target being able to
review at absolute least a days worth of data, and a week would be preferable.  A month's
worth would be ideal.  It would be fantastic if it could be a single binary that could be
run standalone (like using sqlite), but if some sort of TSDB (prometheus, postgres with
extension, influxdb) is needed to achieve this, then a docker compose that can set it up
is fine.  If it can be done without overcomplicating things, the ability to use a sqlite
for simple/low volume setups and a better database for more serious setups would be an
option.  Another consideration is whether all this can be done in a single go process (all
the scheduling and event loops), or whether it needs to be split out into multiple
processes.
